      "writtenOn": "2022-01-28T22:24:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Do we want to try to catch overwrite cases? For example, should do a check like this:\n\n  if (code !\u003d BINDER_EE_CODE_OK \u0026\u0026\n      thread-\u003eextended_error.ee_code !\u003d BINDER_EE_CODE_OK) {\n      /* what should we do? Keep the first extended error?\n       * should we log the fact that we are overriding a previous\n       * error?\n       */\n  }",
      "revId": "8231081adb3515bb77d87c7475a7e17dfb9d6ac6",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a885e81c_e3dd059c",
        "filename": "drivers/android/binder.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 155,
      "author": {
        "id": 1869395
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-01-29T00:20:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We clear the extended error on each ioctl call. So overriding as you mention can only happen within the same ioctl call and I can\u0027t think of a scenario where overriding would _not_ be an error. We usually want to propagate the first error encounter and then exit early. So with that said, I think it makes sense to protect from overriding like in your snippet. Thanks.",
      "parentUuid": "d5a7c755_a71b71d0",
      "revId": "8231081adb3515bb77d87c7475a7e17dfb9d6ac6",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
